// The Spanish NAMES GLOSSARY:  the names people choose in spell programs, in Spanish, shared by every project
// (epic `spanish` P26).
// - `spell lang translate <project> --to es` looks each name up here FIRST:  a name found here costs no `claude` call,
//   and the same English name gets the same Spanish name in every project.
//   - A project's own dictionary, `<Project>.es.names.json5`, may choose otherwise:  the run says so.
// - `spell lang translate <project> --to es --learn` adds a project's names (its types and names) to it.
// - Not spell's own words:  those are the pack's rules, and `GLOSSARY.md` beside this file.
// - Read by node only (`translateProject()`):  nothing imports it, so it never ships to a browser.
//
// Its sections:
// - `names`:  each type's or name's English words, as a program writes them, singular
//   - `name`:  its Spanish, singular
//   - `gender`:  `masculine` | `feminine`, for a noun
//   - `plural`:  only where Spanish's rules get it wrong
//   - `from`:  where it came from;  a better source wins, when two disagree (`--learn` never overwrites one):
//     - `reviewed`:  a person checked it here
//     - `seed:<Project>`:  a translation a person made, e.g. `seed:Solitario`
//     - `learned:<Project>`:  a project's dictionary, by `--learn`
//     - `drafted`:  written by an AI, nobody has checked it yet
// - `phrases`:  general method phrases, with their `(slots)` and `it`, e.g. `turn it face up`
//   - project-specific ones stay in their project's dictionary
// - `genders`:  the gender of common Spanish nouns, by their Spanish, singular,
//   so an article agrees without a guess by the word's ending (`la mano`, `el día`, `la imagen`)
{
  names: {
    // the cards:  Owen's choices in the hand-made Solitario
    ace: { name: "as", gender: "masculine", from: "seed:Solitario" },
    black: { name: "negro", from: "seed:Solitario" },
    card: { name: "carta", gender: "feminine", from: "seed:Solitario" },
